Two manageResponders.spawn(...) sites in requester/spdm_responder_manager.cpp look unsafe — one in the processDiscoveredDevices loop body, one in notifyDeviceAdded. Both have the form:

  manageResponders.spawn([this, device]() -> sdbusplus::async::task<> {
      co_await handleDeviceAdded(device);
      co_return;                                                           
  }());

The lambda is a temporary that's destroyed at the end of the full-expression — spawn(...) returns immediately after taking the coroutine handle. The coroutine frame's this then references the freed lambda, and the captured device is read at the first co_await against memory that's already been returned to the allocator. For a ResponderInfo (which holds a std::variant<MctpResponderInfo, TcpResponderInfo> with std::string inside), this can manifest as std::bad_variant_access: variant is valueless once the freed lambda storage gets reused.                                 

I hit this exact pattern when refactoring locally — wrote a small gtest fixture against SPDMResponderManager (one runtime-add of an MctpResponderInfo, then drain ctx) and got the valueless_by_exception trap reliably until I switched the spawn to                                                                         pass device as a coroutine parameter.   

89179 PS4's SPDMDiscovery::add already uses the safe form for the same reason — would suggest mirroring it here:

manageResponders.spawn(
    [](SPDMResponderManager* self, ResponderInfo dev)
        -> sdbusplus::async::task<> {
        co_await self->handleDeviceAdded(std::move(dev));
        co_return;
    }(this, device));

